// @corpus: platform
// @id: PLATFORM-web-effects
// @category: platform — the web host's effect vocabulary
// @charter: §7.5A, §7.8, §7.9, §9.4, §11
// @milestone: E8
//
// **Every effect the corpus writes, declared.** Architect ruling, 2026-08-07:
//
// > Do not wait for full E9 to declare effect families and operations. […]
// > A wildcard is convenient documentation but terrible semantic identity.
//
// so there are no wildcards here: each entry is one operation with one arity.
//
// The list was MEASURED from the corpus, not recalled. `docs/NEXT.md` carried
// a remembered vocabulary that turned out to include `cache.read`,
// `cache.write`, `observe.*` and `durable.*` — none of which appears in any
// effect row — and to omit `session.read`, `device.query`, `network.subscribe`
// and `unsafe.raw_html`, which do. `docs/RISK_QUEUE.md` 37 is what one missing
// declaration already cost: `LayoutAffect` was written in five files and
// declared nowhere, so a distinction `style.pw` called "the point" rested on a
// spelling no checker could resolve.
//
// Three facts that used to be one string, separated here:
//
//     effect            something computation does
//     capability        the authority required to do it
//     host              one ABI representation of that authority
//
// Not every effect needs a capability, and an effect with `capability none` is
// a declared effect rather than an undeclared one.

module web.effects

// The layout-impact marker, so `impact layout_write when LayoutAffect` names a
// DECLARATION rather than a spelling. An unresolvable marker makes its clause
// inert on purpose — falling back to text is exactly how `LayoutAffect` named
// nothing in five files for three milestones.
import style.{ LayoutAffect }

// **This package exports its effect vocabulary.** Architect ruling,
// 2026-08-07:
//
// > Effect declarations are ambient only because the selected platform package
// > explicitly exports them into the Effect prelude. Their arguments are not
// > ambient.
//
// So a program using this platform writes `!{ database.read<Stores> }` without
// importing anything — but `Stores` still has to be in scope, resolved the way
// any other name is. Only the Effect namespace is exported; Term, Type, Ui and
// Event stay exactly as strict as assumption A-009 made them.
prelude Effect

// --- data ------------------------------------------------------------------

// The type argument names WHICH data, and it is part of the capability's
// identity: `database.read<Stores>` and `database.read<Payments>` are
// different authority, and collapsing them lets a component authorised for one
// reach the other.
//
// ARITY IS UNSETTLED HERE. The corpus writes `database.read` 19 times and
// `database.read<..>` 5 times, and the ruling's test list says a bare
// `database.read` is a wrong-arity error. Declared with the parameter as the
// ruling specifies; the diagnostic that would enforce it is NOT wired, because
// wiring it today reports nineteen working corpus rows. See `docs/NEXT.md`.
effect database.read<T> {
    placement  origin
    capability database.read<T>
}

// Generic, on the architect's ruling of 2026-08-07 — and this is the one place
// so far where the ontology deliberately leads the corpus rather than
// describing it:
//
// > Here I would intentionally not let the existing two bare corpus uses
// > dictate the final ontology. […] A component permitted `database.write<Carts>`
// > should not automatically acquire authority to write Payments, Users,
// > AdminSettings, if the host can enforce that distinction. […] I'd rather
// > start precise.
//
// An earlier version of this file declared `database.write<T>` for symmetry
// with `read` and the arity test rejected it, correctly at the time: the
// declaration described what the vocabulary ought to look like rather than what
// the program said. What changed is not the evidence but the ruling — least
// authority is a design goal the ontology is allowed to express, where symmetry
// alone was not. If E8 later proves a host cannot enforce write authority at
// domain granularity, this collapses on that evidence.
//
// Both bare uses were `Carts.add` and `Carts.clear`, so `<Carts>` is what they
// already meant.
effect database.write<T> {
    placement  origin
    capability database.write<T>
}

// Opening a connection. Architect ruling, 2026-08-07:
//
// > `Database.connect` does not semantically perform `database.read<Database>`.
// > We only wrote that because we lacked the vocabulary to state what it
// > actually does. The ontology now exists specifically so we don't have to lie
// > like that.
//
// It was `database.read<Database>` — "touches the database", with an argument
// naming the module that said so — and the row was load-bearing anyway,
// because `resource` is not a restricted family and without it `connect` was
// placeable in the browser. Making the vocabulary one word larger let the
// program say the true thing instead.
//
// Explicitly NOT `database.read<_>`: a wildcard may be useful later for effect
// polymorphism, and "connect" is not "read any domain".
effect database.connect {
    placement  origin
    capability database.connect
}

// A transaction is not a read or a write: it is the authority to hold one
// open, which is what makes `resource.acquire<DatabaseTransaction>` and its
// matching release checkable as an affine pair.
effect database.transaction {
    placement  origin
    capability database.transaction
}

// --- secrets and session ----------------------------------------------------

// `secret<Payments>` — the capability tag is the argument. Charter §7.8: a
// secret never leaves the origin, and which secret it is decides which
// component may hold it.
effect secret<C> {
    placement  origin
    capability secret<C>
}

// Reading a secret's VALUE, as distinct from holding the capability. The
// corpus writes both, and they are not the same authority.
effect secret.read {
    placement  origin
    capability secret.read
}

// **No intrinsic placement.** Architect ruling, 2026-08-07:
//
// > I don't love deriving `session.read → browser, edge, origin` merely because
// > `Session<A>` may exist there. […] host topology says where a session
// > provider exists.
//
// It declared `browser, edge, origin`, derived from `Label::may_hold`'s rule
// for a session-restricted value — which answers a different question:
//
//     Session<A>      who may observe the value
//     session.read    the authority needed to obtain session state
//
// There is no world where reading session state is meaningless, so there is no
// intrinsic restriction to declare. Which nodes actually provide `session.read`
// is a property of a deployment, and the same program gets a different feasible
// placement under a different topology. That is the point.
effect session.read {
    capability session.read
}

// --- network ----------------------------------------------------------------

effect network.fetch {
    placement  browser, edge, origin
    capability network.fetch
}

effect network.subscribe {
    placement  browser, edge, origin
    capability network.subscribe
}

// --- the document, and charter §7.5A's frame phases -------------------------

// `dom.mutate` and `dom.read` need no host capability: the document is the
// browser world's own, and a component placed there has it by being there.
// Placement is what constrains them — `World::worlds_for` — not a grant.
effect dom.mutate {
    impact     dom_write
    impact     layout_write
    placement  browser
    capability none
}

effect dom.read {
    impact     layout_read
    placement  browser
    capability none
}

// Reading geometry. The effect the whole frame-phase model turns on: doing it
// during render forces a synchronous reflow, and doing it after paint forces a
// second layout for a frame nobody will see.
effect layout.measure {
    impact     layout_read
    placement  browser
    capability none
}

// A style write. The type argument distinguishes one that can invalidate
// layout from one that cannot — charter §7.5A, and the distinction five corpus
// files depend on.
//
// ARITY IS UNSETTLED, as for `database.read`: 7 uses carry `<LayoutAffect>`
// and 2 do not.
effect style.mutate<T> {
    impact     paint_write
    impact     layout_write when LayoutAffect
    placement  browser
    capability none
}

// A compositor animation, which runs without layout. Declared so animating a
// property that invalidates layout is a checkable mistake rather than a slow
// page.
effect animation.composite {
    impact     compositor
    placement  browser
    capability none
}

// A painter. Charter §7.5A gives it inputs precisely so its result can be
// replayed and cached, which is only sound if it is a pure function of them.
effect paint.custom {
    placement  browser
    capability none
}

// After presentation. `intrinsic_effect` in `effects.rs` produces `paint.post`
// for a `post_paint { .. }` block while `A-022` writes `post_paint` in its
// row: two spellings for one thing, recorded rather than reconciled here
// because reconciling them changes what a corpus file says.
effect paint.post {
    placement  browser
    capability none
}

effect post_paint {
    placement  browser
    capability none
}

// --- device ------------------------------------------------------------------

// Real authority, and the reason it is separate from `device.query`: knowing
// where someone is and knowing what their screen can do are not the same ask.
effect device.location {
    placement  browser
    capability device.location
}

effect device.query {
    placement  browser
    capability device.query
}

// --- resources ---------------------------------------------------------------

// The affine pair. The type argument is the resource being acquired, so an
// acquire of one kind and a release of another do not cancel.
effect resource.acquire<R> {
    capability resource.acquire<R>
}

effect resource.release<R> {
    capability resource.release<R>
}

// --- time ---------------------------------------------------------------------

// Split deliberately. A wall-clock read makes an output depend on WHEN it ran,
// so a build-time page or a shared materialization holding one serves an answer
// that was never true for its reader (charter §7.9, §9.4). A monotonic read
// measures a duration and does not.
effect clock.wall {
    capability clock.wall
}

effect clock.read {
    capability none
}

// --- the escape hatches --------------------------------------------------------

// Charter §16: an escape hatch is declared, audited and attributed. Declaring
// them as effects is what makes `unsafe.raw_html` appear in an effect row and
// therefore in a contract, rather than being a syntactic marker a reviewer has
// to notice.
effect unsafe.raw_html {
    capability none
}

effect unsafe.raw_attribute {
    capability none
}

// --- build inputs ---------------------------------------------------------------

// Architect ruling, 2026-08-10, on `include_markdown`:
//
// > It is a **tracked build input operation**. […] The important distinction
// > is: tracked source input read ≠ ambient filesystem I/O. A deterministic
// > build is allowed to depend on input files. What it must not do is silently
// > depend on ambient state that the build graph doesn't know about.
//
// So this is not a filesystem read with the restrictions filed off. It is
// origin-of-truth for a build dependency: the file becomes an explicit input,
// its digest participates in the artifact's identity, and a build that reads it
// is reproducible because the input is named.
//
// `placement build` is the whole point. A page that performs this can only be
// produced at build time — which is what makes A-013's determinism claim mean
// something, rather than being satisfied by an unresolved call contributing
// nothing.
effect build.input.read<T> {
    placement  build
    capability build.input.read<T>
}
